You've got the full Redux setup now — state, actions, and UI component all wired together. This gives you a working cart that persists across components.

Now, you'll want to enhance the UI with quantity controls, shopping cart totals, and order submission. The cart slice handles persistence — add the `persist` middleware to your store configuration and you'll get this for free, even on page refresh.

For the more complex scenarios — cart abandonment recovery, discount coupons — you'd extend this with additional actions and selectors. But this gives you a solid foundation — the core shopping cart pattern with persistence already locked in.

The CartComponent would look something like this (simplified):

```jsx
const CartComponent = () => {
  const { items } = useSelector(state => state.cart);
  const dispatch = useDispatch();

  return (
    <div className="cart">
      {items.map(item => (
        <CartItem 
          item={item}
          onRemove={() => dispatch(removeItem(item.id))}
        />
      ))}
      <CheckoutButton total={items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0)} />
    </div>
  );
};
```

That's the core — the cart item and checkout buttons would be separate components with their own logic. The `CartComponent` is just connecting the Redux state and dispatch to the UI. Access to the Redux store and data is what makes this a full React+Redux component rather than just a UI block.

